Chapter: Arc
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Bike
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Trig

 Project the held-lean, constant-speed arc forward and score how it plays out: off
 a shoulder is a bad lean, net-backward is a disaster, and clearing the forward
 progress is a good lean -- all pinned to the SAME forward, so survivors tie and
 the search then separates them on where they end up across the road.

 THE SCORE IS SHAPED FOR THE SEARCH, not for physics, and reading it as a
 measurement will mislead. `forward` is clamped to `min-forward-progress` precisely
 so that every arm which clears it ties, leaving `end-across` to separate them.
 `frames` is a placeholder on the arms that stayed on the road. This is a verdict
 on a candidate lean, and `Safari chapter LeanSearch` is the only thing entitled to
 read it as one.

 We say:

Section: Leaving the Road

 Shoulder is which shoulder he left, if any.

  Shoulder =
   | ShoulderLeft
   | ShoulderNone
   | ShoulderRight

  ArcOutcome = record {
    shoulder : Shoulder,
    forward : Real,
    crossed : Boolean,
    end-across : Real,
    frames : Real
  }

 The zig uses 1e9 as the frames answer for the arms that did not leave the road,
 and the shoulder brake only reads frames when the shoulder is not none, so the
 value is a placeholder rather than a number. It is kept because it is what the zig
 stores.

  no-frames : Real
  no-frames = 1000000000.0

Section: How Far To Look

  straighten-margin : Real
  straighten-margin = 0.05

  turn-danger-steps : Integer
  turn-danger-steps = 2000

  min-forward-progress : Real
  min-forward-progress = 25.0

Section: Projecting the Arc

  sim-loop : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> ArcOutcome
  sim-loop (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed) (i) (phys) =
    if i >= turn-danger-steps then ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderNone, forward = phys.along - start-along, crossed = crossed, end-across = phys.across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-step start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed i (simulate-rider-step phys 0.0 0.0)

  sim-step : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> ArcOutcome
  sim-step (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed0) (i) (phys) =
    let across = phys.across
    in let forward = phys.along - start-along
    in let crossed = if across * start-side < 0.0 then True else crossed0
    in if across < left-bound then ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderLeft, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if across > right-bound then ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderRight, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if forward < 0.0 then ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderNone, forward = forward, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else if forward >= min-forward-progress then ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderNone, forward = min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-loop start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed (i + 1) phys

 The bounds widen to wherever he already is, so a rider who starts outside the
 inset is not instantly judged to have left the road.

  project-arc : RiderState, Segment -> ArcOutcome
  project-arc (state) (seg) =
    let inset-hw = seg.width / 2.0 - straighten-margin
    in let right-bound = real-max inset-hw state.across
    in let left-bound = real-min (0.0 - inset-hw) state.across
    in sim-loop state left-bound right-bound (r-sign state.across) state.along False 0 state
